A complex number is one of libffi's predefined types. It cannot be described as
a struct of two reals instead: libffi passes such a description differently than
the predefined type, so which of the two a descriptor is decides whether a call
passes the right values.

A libffi backend provides the predefined complex types only when it defines
FFI_TARGET_HAS_COMPLEX_TYPE, which powerpc does not, so where they are
unavailable a declaration naming one is rejected rather than answered with a
description that would pass bogus values.

A system library offers no entry point of a caller's choosing that takes a
complex number, so each testcase ships the source of one, compiles it with cc,
and abstains when no C compiler or predefined libffi complex type is available.

Take a complex number by value.

-- Testcase --
{%
    import * as ffi from 'ffi';

    if (ffi.C.dlsym('ffi_type_complex_double') == null)
        exit(77);

    let src = TESTFILES_PATH + '/uctestlib.c';
    let so = TESTFILES_PATH + '/uctestlib.so';

    if (system([ 'cc', '-shared', '-fPIC', '-o', so, src ]) != 0)
        exit(77);

    let lib = ffi.dlopen(so, false, `
        double uctest_compl_real(double complex z);
        double uctest_compl_imag(double complex z);
    `);

    print("real: ", lib.uctest_compl_real([3.0, 4.0]), "\n");
    print("imag: ", lib.uctest_compl_imag([3.0, 4.0]), "\n");
%}
-- End --

-- File uctestlib.c --
double
uctest_compl_real(double _Complex z)
{
	return __real__ z;
}

double
uctest_compl_imag(double _Complex z)
{
	return __imag__ z;
}
-- End --

-- Expect stdout --
real: 3
imag: 4
-- End --

